(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).afoqt=window.HALCYON_DATA.afoqt||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.afoqt.full||[]).push({
  num: 7, part: `Part Two: The Truth`, title: `After`, weeks: `April 3 to April 9, 2027`,
  tasks: [`PS`,`SJ`,`WK`,`MK`,`TR`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `Saturday, April 3, ten to eight in the morning. Graham had been up since six. Kit could tell by the table.`,
    `He'd cleared it down to the wood. Eleven packets sat in a stack at his end, one for each block, a binder clip on every corner and the subtest written across the top in his black marker. He'd put the test together himself over three weeks of nights, out of library books and every practice page he could find, in the order the Pearson page listed and with the minutes it listed. Beside the stack was a coffee mug of sharpened pencils from his classroom, a pad of scratch paper, and the digital timer from his lab with the magnet on the back.`,
    `Her coat was on the hook by the door, the sheet still folded in the inside pocket where it had ridden for four weeks.`,
    `Graham sat down across from her and slid the first packet over. VERBAL ANALOGIES. He set the timer to eight minutes and pressed the button, and he didn't say go. He'd said what he was going to say on Friday night.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Vern's Nav Table: Physical Science`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Twenty in ten`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"I learned my physics from an airplane, Sergeant, one bad night at a time. That's a slow way to learn it. Here's the short way."</span>`,
          `Physical Science comes eighth, after the inventory and the break. The current Pearson page gives it 20 questions in 10 minutes. Thirty seconds apiece, and the 2015 pamphlet shows five choices on each one. It's high school science. Physics mostly, some chemistry, some earth and sky. Most of it's one step, so you know it or you don't, and if you don't, guess and go. Thirty seconds won't teach you a thing you didn't walk in with. A few want one line of arithmetic, a force or a current or a distance. Do those on scratch paper and look at your units on the way out the door.`,
          `In the 2015 practice pamphlet, Physical Science feeds no composite at all. One newer citation puts it in the CSO composite. Either way, the Pilot composite doesn't lean on it, so it isn't where your Pilot number gets made. It's still twenty questions and ten minutes of your morning, though, and some of it turns up again in Aviation Information, which does count for Pilot. I'll give it to you the way it came to me. Mostly in airplanes, mostly at night.`],
        terms: [[`Physical Science`,`Subtest eight. High school physics, chemistry, earth and sky. Mostly one step.`]],
        exam: `Thirty seconds a question. Don't know it by the time you've read the choices? Guess, and keep the clock for the ones with numbers in them.` },
      { h: `Units first`,
        body: [`I flew in feet and knots and pounds, and this test is in everybody else's units. Science runs on the SI, the International System of Units. Seven base units. Five you'll want cold: the meter for length, the kilogram for mass, the second for time, the kelvin for temperature, the ampere for electric current. The other two are the mole, an amount of a substance, and the candela, which is brightness. Know their names when they walk in.`,
          `Everything else gets built out of those. A newton is the force that speeds one kilogram up by a meter a second, every second. A joule's a newton pushed through a meter, so that's work, or energy. A watt's a joule every second. Power. A pascal's a newton on a square meter. Pressure. Volts and ohms and hertz come later. The miss I see most is people mixing up the jobs. A watt isn't energy, it's energy per second. A kilogram isn't a weight. It's how much stuff there is.`,
          `The prefixes ride on every unit the same. Kilo, a thousand, so a kilometer's 1,000 meters. Mega, a million. Giga, a billion. Going down, centi's a hundredth, milli a thousandth, micro a millionth, nano a billionth. A milliliter is a cubic centimeter, and a liter of water has a mass of a kilogram, and that's the handiest pair of facts in the whole system. A gallon runs about 3.8 liters, if they mix the two on you.`,
          `Try one. How many milligrams in 2.4 grams? A milligram's a thousandth of a gram, so a gram's got a thousand of them. 2,400. Got 0.0024? Then you divided, and you ought to ask yourself whether a smaller unit should give you a smaller count. It shouldn't. Little units, big count.`],
        terms: [[`SI`,`The International System of Units. Meter, kilogram, second, kelvin, ampere, mostly.`],[`Newton, joule, watt, pascal`,`Force, energy, power, pressure. Built out of the base units.`]],
        exam: `Smaller unit, bigger number. Say it once before you convert anything.` },
      { h: `Speed, velocity and falling`,
        body: [`Speed's how far over how long. Velocity's speed with a direction stuck on it, five hundred knots northbound. If it's got a direction it's a vector: velocity, acceleration, force, and the wind in every triangle I ever drew. If it hasn't, it's a scalar. Speed, mass, time, temperature, energy. Acceleration is how fast your velocity changes. Our tankers went from parked to about 160 knots in forty seconds on a cold morning, call it four knots a second, and I felt every one of them in my kidneys. Slowing down counts too, just backward. So does a turn at a steady speed, since the direction keeps changing on you.`,
          `Anything going around a curve needs a pull toward the middle of it. That's centripetal force, center seeking. Swing a bucket on a rope and the rope's doing the pulling. Let go, and the bucket doesn't fly straight out from the middle. It goes off along the edge of the circle, the way it was headed that second. Faster, or a tighter turn, takes more pull. In a bank the wings tip and lend the airplane some of their lift for it, and your seat pushes you around the turn with it. That's the heavy feeling.`,
          `Drop a thing, keep the air off it, and it picks up about 9.8 meters a second of speed, every second. Call it ten if you're in a hurry, and on this test you are. That's g. Speed after a fall, g times the time. Distance, half of g times the time squared. Heavy and light come down together with no air in the way, which nobody believes till they see it. An Apollo crew dropped a hammer and a feather on the moon, on television. Same time. I watched it on my folks' set in the living room, and my dad slapped the side of it twice to keep the picture.`,
          `Try one with 10. Four seconds down, it's doing 40. Meters a second. Distance, half of 10 is 5, 4 squared's 16, call it 80 meters. The miss: somebody takes g times t, the speed, and writes that for the distance. I did it on a quiz in 1972. Speed climbs by the same amount each second. Distance piles up quicker, since each second starts out faster than the last. Square and a half for the distance. Neither for the speed.`],
        terms: [[`Vector`,`Size plus a direction, like velocity or force. Speed, mass and time are scalars. Size only.`],[`Acceleration`,`How fast velocity changes. Slowing down and turning count.`],[`Centripetal force`,`The pull toward the middle that keeps a thing on a curve. Let go and it leaves along the edge.`]],
        exam: `A direction in the answer means a vector. No direction, a scalar.` },
      { h: `Newton's three laws`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Every launch I ever sat through was all three of Newton's laws at once. Somebody should've been selling tickets."</span>`,
          `First law. Inertia. A thing sitting still stays still, and a thing moving keeps going, same speed, straight line, till some force makes it quit. That's your coffee in your lap when the tug stops short. The cup kept going. You didn't. The miss is thinking a moving thing needs pushing to keep moving. It doesn't. It needs a push to change. Out on the ramp friction's doing the stopping, all day long, and that's why it looks the other way.`,
          `Second law, the arithmetic one. Force is mass times acceleration, F equals m a. Same shove, twice the mass, half the acceleration. Weight's just the second law with gravity in it: mass times g. A 90 kilogram pilot in full gear weighs about 880 newtons here. On the moon g's about a sixth as much, so up there he'd weigh about 147, and his mass would still be 90 kilograms. Mass goes where you go. Weight depends on where you're standing. I'd have taken the moon's number at my flight physical. Try one. Net force of 1,800 newtons on a 600 kilogram trailer. Divide. Three meters a second, every second.`,
          `Third law. Forces come in pairs. Push on a thing and it pushes back just as hard, the other way. Four engines throw air and exhaust out the back, and the air throws the jet forward. Kick off the wall at the Y and you push the wall back, and the wall shoves you out into the lane. I swam there winter Tuesdays for years and felt that one every length. The miss is thinking the pair cancels out. It can't, because the two land on two different things, and each one moves its own. So on the test: something that keeps on doing what it was doing, first law. A number for force or mass, second. Two things shoving on each other, third.`],
        terms: [[`Inertia`,`Newton's first law. A thing keeps doing what it's doing till a force changes it.`],[`F = ma`,`Newton's second law. Weight is m times g.`]],
        exam: `Two things pushing on each other is the third law. One thing that won't change what it's doing is the first.` },
      { h: `Momentum, friction and gravity`,
        body: [`Momentum's mass times velocity. P equals m v. It's how hard a thing is to stop. A loaded tanker at taxi speed has more of it than a pickup on the interstate, and I never saw a tug driver argue with one. When the pieces only push on each other, a crash or a push apart, total momentum after is what it was before. Conservation of momentum.`,
          `Try one. A 4 kilogram cart rolls 3 meters a second into a 2 kilogram cart sitting still, and they latch. Before, 4 times 3. Twelve. After, still twelve, spread over 6 kilograms. Two meters a second. The miss is averaging the speeds, 3 and 0, and saying a meter and a half. Momentum counts the mass. Speeds don't average, and I'd write that on the back of my hand if I were you.`,
          `Friction pushes back on sliding. It depends on the two surfaces, and how hard they're pressed together, and how much area's touching hardly matters, which surprises everybody, me included. Getting a crate started usually takes more than keeping it going, since the friction on a thing sitting still runs higher than on one that's sliding. Any mover will tell you that, and charge you for it. I watched six of us get a dead tug rolling once on a January ramp, and two could keep it going after. Friction makes heat out of motion. Feel a brake after a long taxi and you'll believe it.`,
          `Gravity pulls every mass toward every other mass. Double the distance between two things and the pull drops to a quarter. Triple it, a ninth. That's the inverse square. The miss is halving it when the distance doubles, and I've seen colonels do it. Newton worked all that out without ever setting foot in an airplane, which has always bothered me a little. That same pull is what drags the oceans into tides, and I'll get to those.`],
        terms: [[`Momentum`,`Mass times velocity. Same total before a collision and after.`],[`Inverse square`,`Twice as far, a quarter the pull. Three times, a ninth.`]],
        exam: `Doubling the distance quarters gravity's pull. Don't halve it.` },
      { h: `Pressure, Pascal and floating`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Pressure kept me alive at thirty thousand feet. The cabin held it in and the altimeter read it, and I never saw a lick of it."</span>`,
          `Pressure's force spread over area. Pressure equals force divided by area. Same force, smaller area, more pressure, which is why a sharp knife cuts and a snowshoe doesn't sink. In a liquid it grows with depth, and with how dense the liquid is. It doesn't care one bit how wide the tank is. Fresh water adds about one pound per square inch every 2.31 feet down, about one atmosphere every ten meters. Air works the same way, only we live at the bottom of it. At sea level, about 14.7 pounds on every square inch, thinning as you climb. An altimeter's just a barometer with feet painted on its face, and I trusted mine more than some pilots.`,
          `Pascal's principle. Squeeze a fluid that's shut up in a line, and every inch of that line feels the squeeze, the same. Small piston on one end, big one on the other, and you've built a force multiplier. Force out over force in is big area over small area. Car jack, barber's chair, the brakes on your truck, and the chair at my dentist's that I'd rather not think about. The bill comes due in distance. The big piston moves less, same ratio, so what comes out in work never beats what went in. It only works because a liquid hardly squeezes. Get air in a brake line and the pedal goes soft. My first car was a '61 Falcon with a leak in the line, and I learned Pascal pumping that pedal down a hill outside Sedalia.`,
          `Try one. Say a shop press has a small piston of 2 square inches and a big one of 40. Lean on the small one with 50 pounds. That's 25 pounds per square inch all through the fluid, so the big one pushes 25 times 40. A thousand pounds. Push the small one down 10 inches and the big one comes up half an inch. Twenty times the force, a twentieth the travel, and no free lunch.`,
          `Floating's Archimedes. Whatever's in the water gets pushed up by the weight of the water it shoved aside. That's the whole rule. Shove aside its own weight before it's all the way under, and it floats. It comes down to density. Less dense than the fluid, it floats. More, it sinks. The share riding under the surface is its density over the fluid's. Ice is about nine tenths as dense as water, so nine tenths of an iceberg's under. Ask the Titanic. The miss is heavy things sink. A steel ship floats. It's the whole shape that counts, air inside and all.`],
        terms: [[`Pressure`,`Force over area. In a liquid, depth and density. Never width.`],[`Pascal's principle`,`Squeeze a shut in fluid and the squeeze goes everywhere. Hydraulics trade distance for force.`],[`Buoyancy`,`The push up on a thing in a fluid. It equals the weight of fluid shoved aside.`]],
        exam: `Pressure's per square inch. When they offer you a wider tank, ask how deep, not how big.` },
      { h: `Gases`,
        body: [`A gas has no shape and no set volume. It fills whatever you put it in, and you can squeeze it, which you can't do to a liquid worth a darn. Three gas laws, one idea. Boyle's law: squeeze a gas, hold the temperature, and the pressure climbs in step. Half the volume, twice the pressure. Charles's law: warm a gas, hold the pressure, and it grows in step with its temperature in kelvins. A hot air balloon is Charles's law with a basket under it. I rode in one at the state fair in 1988, and the man flying it never said kelvin once. Warm one that's sealed in something rigid and the pressure goes up the same way. That's why a tire reads high after a long run, and why we checked ours cold, at four in the morning, with a flashlight in our teeth.`,
          `The trap's the temperature. These laws run on kelvins, never Celsius. To get kelvins, add 273 to the Celsius. Take a balloon from 20 Celsius to 40. That isn't double. In kelvins it's 293 to 313, about seven percent more, and that's all the balloon grows. Plenty of people double the Celsius, and they double the balloon right along with it.`],
        terms: [[`Boyle's law`,`Same temperature, squeeze a gas to half and the pressure doubles.`],[`Charles's law`,`Same pressure, the volume follows the temperature, counted in kelvins.`]],
        exam: `A gas law with a temperature in it? Kelvins first, before anything else.` },
      { h: `Work, energy and power`,
        body: [`Work is a force times how far it moves something, in the direction it pushed. Joules again. Push a cart 8 meters with 15 newtons. That's 120 joules. Hold a box over your head an hour and you'll be wore out and you'll have done no work on it at all, since it didn't go anywhere. Walk it across the room at the same height, and by the book the force holding it up did nothing either. That force pointed up. The box went sideways. I argued with a nav school instructor about that once, and lost.`,
          `Energy does the work. Kinetic energy's energy of motion: half the mass, times the speed squared. Double the speed and you've got four times the energy, which is why a car at 60 takes about four times the braking distance it takes at 30. Potential energy's energy of position. Mass times g times height. Lift a 20 kilogram toolbox 2 meters and you've stored about 392 joules in it. Call it 400. Let go of it and that height turns back into speed on the way down, and then into a dent in the floor.`,
          `Energy doesn't get made and it doesn't get lost. It changes clothes, that's all. Conservation of energy. A roller coaster trades height for speed and back again, and friction skims a little off as heat every hill, which is why the second hill's lower. Power's how fast the work gets done. Work over time, in watts. Lift that toolbox in 2 seconds, about 200 watts. Take 4, and it's 100. Same work, half the power. The miss is mixing work with power, how much with how fast. A horsepower's about 746 watts, if they ask.`],
        terms: [[`Kinetic energy`,`Half m v squared. Twice the speed, four times the energy.`],[`Potential energy`,`m g h. Energy for being up high.`],[`Power`,`Work over time. Watts.`]],
        exam: `Twice the speed, four times the energy. The speed's squared in there.` },
      { h: `Simple machines`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Every crew chief I ever knew was a physicist nobody'd told. A longer handle on a stuck bolt is a lever."</span>`,
          `A machine never gives you work for free. It trades. Less force over more distance, or the other way around. How much it multiplies your force is the mechanical advantage, load over effort. The six simple machines: lever, pulley, wheel and axle, inclined plane, wedge, screw.`,
          `A lever turns on a fulcrum. Effort times its distance from the fulcrum equals load times its distance. Put 300 newtons half a meter from the fulcrum, push a meter and a half out the other side, and you need 100 newtons. A hundred times one and a half balances three hundred times a half. Your hand goes three times as far as the load. Three classes, named for what's in the middle. Fulcrum in the middle, first class: seesaw, pry bar, pliers. Load in the middle, second class: wheelbarrow, nutcracker. Effort in the middle, third class: tweezers, a broom, your own forearm. Third class costs you force and buys you speed.`,
          `Pulleys. One fixed pulley only turns your pull around, advantage of one, and you lift by hauling down. A movable pulley rides up with the load, so two strands hold it. Advantage of two. With a block and tackle, count the strands of rope holding up the moving block. Four strands, advantage four. A hundred newtons of pull raises 400, and you haul in four meters of rope for every meter it rises. Miss: counting pulleys instead of strands. Or counting the strand in your hand when it comes down off a fixed pulley overhead. That one's holding up nothing but your arm.`,
          `A ramp's an inclined plane, and its advantage is length over height. A 6 meter ramp up to a dock 1.5 meters high, advantage of 4. A 1,200 newton barrel takes about 300 to push up, friction aside, over four times the distance. A wedge is two ramps back to back. Axe head. A screw's a ramp wound around a post. A wheel and axle, a steering wheel or a screwdriver handle, multiplies by the wheel's radius over the axle's.`,
          `Gears make the same trade. A small gear driving a big one turns it slower and harder. Ten teeth driving forty turns the big one once for every four turns of the little one, with about four times the turning force. Swap them and you buy speed with force. Every one of these is the same deal as the hydraulics. Force times distance in, force times distance out, less a little for friction. Nobody's ever beaten it, and a fellow at the county fair in 1964 sold my dad a gadget that said it could.`],
        terms: [[`Mechanical advantage`,`Load over effort. You pay for force in distance.`],[`Lever classes`,`What's in the middle: fulcrum, first. Load, second. Effort, third.`]],
        exam: `Count rope strands holding the load, not pulleys. On any machine, more force means less distance.` },
      { h: `Heat and temperature`,
        body: [`Temperature's how hard the particles in a thing are jiggling, on average. Heat's energy on its way from something hotter to something colder. A tub of warm bathwater holds more heat than a cup of boiling water, though the cup's hotter. Three scales. Celsius puts water freezing at 0 and boiling at 100. Fahrenheit, 32 and 212. Kelvin starts at absolute zero, where the jiggling's as low as it goes. That's 0 kelvins, about minus 273 Celsius, and nothing gets colder. Its degrees are the same size as Celsius. Kelvin is Celsius plus 273. For Fahrenheit, Celsius times nine fifths, plus 32. Twenty Celsius: 36, plus 32, 68. I did that in my head for thirty years of weather briefings and got it wrong maybe twice that anybody caught. Minus 40 is the one spot where the two agree, and I've stood a ramp at it with my eyelashes frozen.`,
          `Heat gets around three ways. Conduction's contact, molecule to molecule, like a wrench left out on the ramp in July. Grab it. You'll learn. Convection's a fluid carrying it, warm air or water rising and cool sinking and around again, the way one radiator heats a whole room. Radiation needs nothing in between. It's light and infrared crossing empty space, and it's how the sun's heat gets here. The miss is calling a campfire's warmth on your face convection. The hot air's headed straight up. What's on your face is radiation.`,
          `Things grow when they warm and shrink when they cool. Most things, anyhow. Thermal expansion. It's the gaps in a bridge deck that go thump under your tires, and it's why a gallon of cold fuel weighs more than a gallon of warm. Water's the oddball. Densest at about 4 Celsius, and it gets bigger when it freezes, so ice floats. A lake freezes top down. That's the only reason a single fish in Missouri lives to see March.`,
          `Specific heat. How much heat it takes to warm a thing up. Water's runs high. Takes a pile of heat to warm it, gives a pile back cooling off, so a town on a big lake runs cooler all summer and warmer into the fall than a town fifty miles back from the shore. We flew out of a desert base one summer where the ramp hit a hundred and ten by three in the afternoon and you wanted a jacket at four in the morning. And when a thing changes state, every bit of the heat goes into the change. The temperature just sits. A pot of ice water, stirred, stays at zero till the last cube's gone. People expect the thermometer to climb. It waits.`],
        terms: [[`Heat and temperature`,`Temperature's how fast the particles go, on average. Heat's energy headed from hot to cold.`],[`Conduction, convection, radiation`,`Touch, a moving fluid, waves across empty space.`],[`Specific heat`,`How much heat it takes to warm a thing. Water takes a lot.`]],
        exam: `Kelvin is Celsius plus 273. Heat that crosses empty space can only be radiation.` },
      { h: `Matter and how it changes`,
        body: [`Matter comes in states. Solid keeps its shape and its volume. Liquid keeps its volume and takes the shape of whatever it's in. Gas takes both from the container. Heat a gas enough and it splits into charged pieces, a plasma. Lightning. The sun. And the blue fire I once watched crawl over our windscreen for twenty minutes above Kansas. St. Elmo's fire. The copilot said a word I won't repeat. It didn't hurt a thing.`,
          `The changes have names, and the test likes names. Solid to liquid, melting. Liquid to solid, freezing. Liquid to gas is vaporization, evaporating off the top or boiling all the way through. Gas to liquid's condensation, the sweat on a cold glass. Solid straight to gas, sublimation, like a mothball shrinking away in a closet, or snow going thin on a cold dry day without ever getting wet. Gas straight to solid, deposition, frost on a windshield. Boiling depends on the pressure on top. Lower it and water boils cooler. Raise it and water boils hotter, which is the whole trick inside a pressure cooker.`,
          `Density's mass over volume. Water's 1 gram a cubic centimeter. A kilogram a liter. About 8.34 pounds a gallon. A 600 gram block that takes up 1,000 cubic centimeters comes out 0.6, so it floats, six tenths under. Surface tension is water hanging onto itself at the top. It lets a needle lie on the surface and a water strider walk, and it's why a drop stands up round instead of going flat. Water holding on to water is cohesion. Water holding on to something else, glass or cloth or a paper towel, is adhesion. When the stick to the wall beats the stick to itself, water climbs a skinny tube on its own, and that's capillary action. It's how a paper towel drinks a spill and how a tree gets water up out of the ground. In a narrow glass tube water's surface curves up at the edges. Mercury's curves down, since mercury would rather hold on to itself.`,
          `A physical change leaves the stuff what it was. Melting, bending, sugar in your coffee. A chemical change makes something new. Burning, rusting, baking. Signs it's chemical: a gas you didn't have before, a color change, heat or light given off, a solid falling out of a clear liquid. Mass isn't made or lost either way. Burn a log and the ash weighs less, sure, but count the smoke and the gases and it all adds back up.`],
        terms: [[`Sublimation and deposition`,`Solid straight to gas. Gas straight to solid.`],[`Density`,`Mass over volume. Water's 1 gram a cubic centimeter.`],[`Cohesion, adhesion, capillary action`,`Sticking to itself. Sticking to something else. Water climbing a narrow tube when adhesion wins.`],[`Chemical change`,`Something new comes out of it. Burning, rusting.`]],
        exam: `Solid straight to gas is sublimation. Gas straight to solid is deposition. They'll offer you both.` },
      { h: `Atoms, the table and bonds`,
        body: [`Chemistry was my worst grade in college, a C, and the man was being generous. So here it is the way I finally got it. An atom's a small heavy nucleus with electrons around it. In the nucleus you've got protons, a plus charge each, and neutrons, no charge at all. Around it, electrons, a minus charge each, light as nothing. If the nucleus were a marble on the fifty yard line, the electrons would be gnats up in the cheap seats. The count of protons is the atomic number, and it decides the element. Six is carbon. Always. Pencil lead, coal, a diamond, every one of them six. Eight is oxygen. Protons plus neutrons is the mass number. Same element, different count of neutrons, is an isotope. Gain or lose electrons and you've got an ion, a charged atom. Lose one, it's plus. Gain one, minus.`,
          `Try one. Eleven protons, 12 neutrons. Atomic number 11. That's sodium. Mass number 23. Lose an electron and it's a sodium ion, plus one. The miss: changing the protons and calling it an ion. Change the protons and it isn't the same element anymore. It's somebody else.`,
          `The periodic table runs by atomic number, left to right, top to bottom. A row's a period. A column's a group. Everything in a group has the same count of electrons in its outer shell, and that's why they behave like brothers. The far left column, under hydrogen, is the alkali metals. Drop a chunk of sodium in water and stand back. My high school chemistry teacher did it off the river bridge in 1968, and they talked about it in town for a month. One column in from the right edge are the halogens, chlorine and fluorine, each one hungry for one more electron. The right edge itself is the noble gases, full up and quiet, like the heritage room on a Saturday. Metals sit on the left and through the middle, and they conduct. Nonmetals sit off to the right.`,
          `Atoms hang on to each other with bonds, and there's two kinds worth knowing. Ionic: one atom hands electrons to another, usually a metal to a nonmetal, and the two charged ions cling. Table salt. Covalent: two nonmetals share electrons. Water, carbon dioxide, the fuel in a tanker. An element's one kind of atom. A compound's two or more kinds bonded in a fixed recipe, water always two hydrogens to one oxygen. A mixture is things together but not bonded, any amounts, and you can pull them apart without chemistry. Salt water. Air. Gravel. Boil off the water and the salt's still in the pan, which I know because I've burned a pan that way.`],
        terms: [[`Atomic number`,`The proton count. It picks the element.`],[`Isotope and ion`,`Isotope, different neutrons. Ion, electrons gained or lost.`],[`Group`,`A column on the periodic table. Same outer shell, same habits.`]],
        exam: `Protons pick the element. Neutrons pick the isotope. Electrons pick the charge.` },
      { h: `Acids, bases and the nucleus`,
        body: [`The pH scale runs 0 to 14. Seven's neutral, pure water. Under 7 is acid, lemon juice and vinegar and what's in a battery. Over 7 is base: baking soda, ammonia, the bleach a water plant feeds. Each whole step is ten times. A pH of 2 is ten times more acidic than 3, a hundred times more than 4, and a thousand times more than 5. Mix an acid with a base and they drag each other toward the middle, and you get a salt and water. Neutralization. It's why you'd put baking soda on spilled battery acid.`,
          `Radioactive atoms break down on their own, steady as a dripping faucet. Half life's just the time it takes for half of a sample to go. Then half of whatever's left goes in the next stretch, and so on down. Start with 96 grams, and say the half life's 2 hours. After two hours you've got 48. Four hours, 24. Six, 12. At eight hours there's 6 left, and it never does get all the way to nothing. The miss is thinking it's all gone after two half lives. Half of a half isn't zero. It's a quarter, and a quarter's still sitting there.`,
          `What comes flying off a breaking atom is radiation, and they'll want three names. Alpha's a slow, heavy lump, two protons and two neutrons, and a sheet of paper stops it cold. Beta's a fast electron. A thin sheet of aluminum'll do for that one. Gamma's a ray, light's big cousin, and you want thick lead or a wall of concrete between you and it. My dad's wristwatch had a radium dial that glowed green, and he wore it to bed for thirty years. Getting energy out of a nucleus on purpose, there's two ways. Fission splits a heavy one, uranium, into two smaller pieces. That's the power plant down by Fulton. Fusion jams light ones, hydrogen, together into something heavier, and that's the sun. Split heavy, fuse light.`],
        terms: [[`pH`,`0 to 14. Under 7 acid, over 7 base. Each step's ten times.`],[`Half life`,`How long till half the sample's gone.`],[`Alpha, beta, gamma`,`A lump of two protons and two neutrons, paper stops it. A fast electron, thin metal stops it. A ray, it takes lead.`],[`Fission and fusion`,`A heavy nucleus split. Light nuclei joined. A power plant, and the sun.`]],
        exam: `One pH step is ten times. Count the steps and multiply the tens. Never add them.` },
      { h: `Electricity and magnets`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"I never touched a wire on that airplane. I knew which breakers to pull, and I knew why."</span>`,
          `Charge comes in plus and minus. Like charges push apart. Unlike pull together. Rub a comb on a wool sweater and electrons hop from one to the other, and now the comb's charged. Static. The protons stay home in the nucleus, so it's always the electrons doing the traveling. Conductors let charge flow. Metals, copper for most wiring, and salty water. Insulators don't. Rubber, glass, plastic, dry wood.`,
          `Current's the flow of charge, in amperes. Voltage is the push behind it, in volts. Resistance is how hard the path pushes back, in ohms. The avionics shop had it painted on the wall in letters a foot high, and I never saw anybody look at it. Ohm's law ties the three together. Volts equal amps times ohms, so amps are volts over ohms, and if that won't stay put, draw the triangle the way the boys in avionics did. Put a 6 volt lantern battery across a 3 ohm bulb and you get 2 amps, enough to find your keys by. Power's volts times amps. Watts. The toaster in our kitchen pulls 10 amps off 120 volts. That's 1,200 watts, and it still burns one side of the bread.`,
          `There's two ways to hook things up. Series is one road, and everything's strung out on it like ducks crossing the river road. The same current runs through all of it, the ohms add, and if one bulb quits the road's cut and they all go dark. Runway edge lights run on a loop like that, with a little transformer at every fixture so one dead lamp won't black out the whole side, and somebody learned that the hard way before my time. Parallel's separate roads, side by side, and every one of them gets the whole voltage. Add up the branches and you've got what the battery's putting out. Now here's the part that sounds wrong. The total resistance comes out lower than any one branch. You opened another road, same as they did on the beltway in '79, and traffic moved better, not worse. Two equal resistors side by side, you get half of one. My mother's Christmas lights were series, and every December my dad stood on a kitchen chair on Christmas Eve, cussing, testing bulbs one at a time. Your house is parallel, which is why the fridge keeps running when the bulb in the hall goes. Fuses and breakers go in series, out front of whatever they protect, so when one pops, everything behind it quits.`,
          `Try one. Two 10 ohm resistors on 20 volts. Hook them in series and it's 20 ohms, so 1 amp. Side by side it's 5 ohms and 4 amps, two down each branch. Folks add resistances that sit side by side all the time, and I did it on a trainer board myself in 1974. Count the roads before you add a thing.`,
          `Batteries push current one way, like a one way street downtown, and that's direct current, DC. Your wall outlet flips it back and forth sixty times a second. Alternating current, AC, and that's the hum you hear off the gray can on a pole on a hot night. That can's a transformer. Two coils of wire wound on one iron core, and the trick is the turns. More turns on the side going out than coming in, it steps the volts up. Fewer, it steps them down. The power company runs it across the county at thousands of volts because it wastes less that way, and the can by your house knocks it down to 120 for the toaster. Only works on AC, though. I watched an airman hook one to a battery on a trainer board once, and the sergeant let him go at it for an hour.`,
          `Current makes magnetism too. Wind wire into a coil, run current through it, there's your electromagnet. More turns, more current, iron in the middle, and it's stronger. Kill the current and it lets go, and that's how the crane at the junkyard on Route 9 drops a car. Run the idea backward and you've got a generator. Shove a magnet through a coil and current flows. Magnets have a north end and a south. Like ends shove apart. Unlike ends grab. The earth's one big magnet. A compass needle's a little one, lining itself up. Ours rode up on the glare shield in a little bowl of fluid. Swung like a drunk in every bank. Whiskey compass, the pilots called it. It never quit on me. I can't say that for the Doppler.`],
        terms: [[`Ohm's law`,`Volts equal amps times ohms. Draw the triangle, V on top.`],[`Series and parallel`,`One road, ohms add. Side by side roads, full voltage on each.`],[`AC and DC`,`Back and forth, from the wall. One way, from a battery. A transformer changes AC voltage only.`]],
        exam: `Side by side is parallel. Full push on every branch, and the ohms go down.` },
      { h: `Waves, sound and light`,
        body: [`A wave carries energy along without hauling the water off with it. Sit on a dock at Lake of the Ozarks and watch a swell go under your bobber. The bobber goes up, goes down, and stays right where it was. Crest to crest is the wavelength. How many go by in a second is the frequency, in hertz. How tall they are is the amplitude. Speed is frequency times wavelength. A sound at 340 meters a second and 680 hertz is half a meter long. Same speed, higher frequency, shorter waves. A piccolo throws short ones. A tuba throws long ones.`,
          `Sound's a push wave. Squeeze, stretch, squeeze, all through whatever it's in, so it has to be in something. No air, no sound. Out in space a shout goes nowhere at all. About 340 meters a second in air, faster in water, faster again in steel. Pitch is frequency. Loud is amplitude. An echo's sound bouncing back. Coming at you, the noise bunches its waves up and the pitch climbs. Going away, it drops. Every freight train through Tolliver does it. That's the Doppler effect, and it's how the Doppler radar on my airplane read our ground speed. Until the day it didn't, and then I did it with a pencil.`,
          `Light's an electromagnetic wave, and it needs nothing at all to travel through. About 300,000 kilometers a second. 186,000 miles. The electromagnetic waves make one long family, longest wave and lowest frequency first: radio, microwaves, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X rays, gamma rays. Visible runs red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet. Red's the longest wave, violet the shortest. The miss is setting ultraviolet below visible because you can't see it. It's above. More frequency, more energy, and that's why it burns you on a ramp in June.`,
          `Light bounces. Light bends. Off a mirror, it leaves at the same angle it hit, measured off a line straight out of the glass. Reflection. Into glass or water, it changes speed and bends. Refraction. Stand a fence post in a creek and it looks snapped off right at the waterline. A prism bends each color its own amount and fans white light into a rainbow, and Patrice has one hanging in the kitchen window that throws them on the ceiling every afternoon about four. Fat in the middle, convex, a lens pulls light together to a point. That's your magnifying glass. I set a pile of leaves smoking with one in 1959, and my mother had words with me. Thin in the middle, concave, it spreads light out. Every star I shot low through that sextant port sat a hair higher than it really was, because the air bent it on the way in. There was a table for that. There was a table for everything.`],
        terms: [[`Frequency and wavelength`,`Waves a second, and crest to crest. Multiply them for the speed.`],[`Electromagnetic spectrum`,`Radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X ray, gamma. Frequency going up.`],[`Reflection and refraction`,`Bouncing off at the same angle. Bending where light changes speed.`]],
        exam: `Sound needs air or water or steel to ride on. Light doesn't need a thing. Up the family means more frequency, more energy, shorter waves.` },
      { h: `Earth and sky`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Navigators were about the last fellows paid to look up. Learn the sky like you learned that ramp."</span>`,
          `Air's layered. Bottom one's the troposphere, from the ground up to about 8 kilometers over the poles and 18 over the equator, and near all the weather's in it, which is why I spent my working life staring at it. It cools as you climb, about 2 degrees Celsius every thousand feet, so the top of a thunderhead runs colder than your freezer. Next up, the stratosphere, with the ozone in it. Long range jets like to cruise near its floor, where the ride smooths out. Above that the mesosphere, where meteors burn up. Then the thermosphere, with the northern lights in it, then the exosphere, thinning out to nothing at all. The air's about 78 percent nitrogen and 21 oxygen. I kept that on a card in my flight suit, and I couldn't tell you why.`,
          `Air moves from high pressure toward low. A high's sinking air, and sinking air dries out, so a high's mostly clear. A low is rising air, and rising air cools and clouds up and rains. So a falling barometer means a low's on its way. A front's where two air masses meet. Cold front shoves under the warm air fast and steep: a narrow line of thunderstorms, gusts, then cooler clearer air behind. I rode through one over Nebraska in 1984 and wore a whole thermos of coffee home on my flight suit. Warm front slides up over the cold slow: a long gray spell of low clouds and steady rain. Relative humidity is the water the air's holding over the most it could hold at that temperature. Cool the air and the same water's a bigger share, so the humidity climbs. At 100 percent you've hit the dew point, and the water condenses. That's a cloud. On the ground, fog. I sat out more fog in Kansas City than I care to count. Dispatch ran on fronts. I'd hold a freighter forty minutes for a cold front, take an earful from a man in a tie, and then watch the line come through right on time and blow his coffee off the ramp.`,
          `The wind doesn't run straight from high to low, either. The earth turns under it, and that bends moving air to the right up here and to the left south of the equator. That's the Coriolis effect, and it's why our lows wind up counterclockwise. Up near the top of the troposphere there's a river of fast wind called the jet stream, mostly running west to east, a hundred knots and better in winter. Eastbound we rode it. Westbound we fought it, and you could read which in the fuel log.`,
          `Earth spins on its axis once a day, and that's your day and night. Goes around the sun once a year. Spin's rotation, the trip around's revolution, and I've watched grown men swap those two at a briefing. The axis leans about 23 and a half degrees, and the lean's what makes seasons. The half tipped toward the sun gets it higher overhead and the long days. Distance hasn't got a thing to do with it. We're closest to the sun in early January, and you've stood on a Missouri ramp in January. Patrice still says summer's when we're closer, and I let her.`,
          `Moon's around us about once a month. One night in the den I showed Patrice's nephew with the shade off a lamp and an orange on a pencil. Hold it out, turn slow. Half that orange stays lit, always. What you get from where you stand is some of the lit half, a sliver, a half, all of it, back down. Phases. It's not our shadow. Our shadow on the moon's an eclipse, and that makes the paper, lunar at a full moon. A solar eclipse is the moon parked in front of the sun, new moon. The boy ate the orange. Tides come off the moon's pull, mostly. It raises two bulges in the ocean, one on the side facing it and one on the far side, so most coasts get two high tides a day, and they're biggest when sun and moon line up, at new and full. A navigator who'd crossed the Pacific told me he could tell the date by the beach at Guam. I believed him for years.`,
          `The sun's a star. Middling, as stars go. Out from it, Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, then Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune. My Very Educated Mother Just Served Us Nachos, if you want the order on your scratch paper. First four are rock. Last four are big balls of gas and ice, and Jupiter's the biggest thing out there but the sun. Sunlight takes about eight minutes to get here. If it quit on us, we'd have eight minutes before anybody knew.`,
          `Under your boots there's a thin rind of rock, the crust. Under that the mantle, solid rock that creeps a few centimeters a year. Then the outer core, liquid iron and nickel, the only layer that pours. Then a solid inner core, solid only because it's squeezed so hard. The crust's cracked into plates that drift about as fast as your fingernails grow, and the earthquakes and volcanoes bunch up along the cracks. Rock comes three ways. Igneous cooled from melted rock. Obsidian is lava that cooled so fast it went to black glass, and pumice is lava so full of gas it'll float in a bucket. Sedimentary is layers pressed flat, sandstone, limestone, and that's where you find the fossils. Metamorphic got cooked by heat and pressure without melting. Marble from limestone. Slate from shale. There's a slate chalkboard in the heritage room older than I am.`],
        terms: [[`Troposphere`,`The bottom layer of air. Near all the weather.`],[`Relative humidity`,`Water in the air over the most it could hold. At 100 percent you're at the dew point.`],[`Coriolis and the jet stream`,`Earth's spin bends winds right up here. A fast river of wind up high, west to east.`],[`Rotation and revolution`,`The spin, a day. The trip around the sun, a year.`],[`Igneous, sedimentary, metamorphic`,`Cooled melt. Pressed layers. Cooked by heat and pressure.`]],
        exam: `Rotation is a day and revolution is a year. Phases are the angle, and eclipses are the shadow.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f7s1`, domain:`Verbal`, task:`WK`, title:`Twelve seconds a word`, q:`DISSEMBLE: closest in meaning. Which one does Kit mark?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const a6=((all||{})[6]||{}).picks||{},tn=(G||{}).toldnoone;return [
        ...(a6[11]===0?[`Friday night she'd gone back in with the drill and asked him straight what was under the tarp. Graham had taken the drill out of her hand and put two new screws in the whiteboard bracket. ${tn?`<span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Quiet, careful, warm.">"Tomorrow. After the test."</span>`:`<span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Quiet, careful, warm.">"Tomorrow. After yours."</span>`} Then he'd gone up to bed. He'd handed her an after back, and she'd let him.`]:[]),
        tn?`Behind him on the fridge, Saturday's square said PRACTICE TEST, 5 HRS, ME ON THE CLOCK, in green.`:`Behind him on the fridge, Saturday's square said PRACTICE TEST, 5 HRS, ME ON THE CLOCK, in green, and under that, small, in his black, 2019.`,
        `The analogies went the way they went at Vern's. At eight minutes the timer beeped. <span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Even, plain, neutral.">"Time."</span>`,
        `He took the packet, slid the next one over, and set the timer for twenty nine. Arithmetic: pump rates, fuel, a train out of Kansas City, and she was quicker at those than she'd ever been. Then WORD KNOWLEDGE, twenty five in five minutes. Twelve seconds a word.`,
        `Number fourteen was DISSEMBLE. She knew the word. She'd known it since high school, and she'd never liked it.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Take something apart.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She marked it and was two words on before it caught up with her. Take apart had an A in the middle of it. Disassemble. She'd taken apart more things than she could count, and this word wasn't one of them.`,
          why:`That's disassemble, the crew chief's word, wearing this one's coat. The letters overlap enough to fool a tired eye at twelve seconds a word. Read the word that's on the page, not the one you use every day.`},
        {t:`Put on a false front.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`She marked it and went on. To dissemble was to keep a face in front of the truth, and you could do it without saying one false word. She'd stayed for the inspection. Boyd had asked her to. All of it true, and all of it standing in front of a nineteen. She was on fifteen before Graham's timer had moved much.`,
          why:`Dissemble. To hide what's real behind a face, put on a front. Closest in meaning is whichever one fits every place the word goes. It's from the old Latin for disguising yourself, same root as similar. Looking like what you aren't. Then don't trust it to look like its cousins, because one of them is a trap with a wrench in it. Know it, or take your best root guess and get on to the next.`},
        {t:`Tell a flat lie.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Close, and it held her three seconds she didn't have. A liar says a false thing. This word didn't need anybody to say anything false. She changed it and went on, and the three seconds went with her.`,
          why:`Close, and the near miss is the lesson. A liar says what isn't so. Somebody who dissembles can say nothing but true things and still keep you from what's real. On this subtest a near meaning will often sit right next to the right one. Pick the one that fits the word all the way across.`},
        {t:`Spread something around.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Spread around was disseminate, another word with the same first letters. She'd mixed those two up in high school, and her teacher had written them side by side on the board.`,
          why:`Disseminate means to scatter or spread. Starts the same way, means something else. Look at the whole word, the middle especially. Both cousins turn up in the choices sometimes. The people who write these know all about them.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s2`, domain:`Quantitative`, task:`MK`, title:`The circle in the square`, q:`A circle she can't see into, and the clock running. What should Kit do with number fourteen?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const st=(G||{}).skippedtimer;return [`Then MATH KNOWLEDGE, twenty five in twenty two minutes. Graham set the timer and sat back with his coffee. Out the window the neighbor's dog was digging at something by the fence again.`,
        st?`She'd worked Vern's four sets with no clock, every one of them right, every one of them slow. She'd never heard the egg timer go off over her shoulder. Now she did them the way she'd done them at his table, in order, every step written down, and at eleven minutes she was on number nine. Graham's timer couldn't have cared less how careful she was.`:`She'd run Vern's four sets on his egg timer, twenty two minutes each, and gone over her misses afterward with no clock at all. She knew where her time went now. At eleven minutes she was on number twelve, about where she wanted to be.`,
        `Number fourteen. Circle in a square, and they wanted the corners. She looked at it, and it didn't open.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Leave it blank, and come back to it if there's time.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She skipped it and kept going. She meant to come back. Whether she got there was up to the clock, and a blank on a sheet like Graham's was just a wrong answer she hadn't bothered to make.`,
          why:`Right to move on. Wrong to leave it empty. Your score's built from the number right, and a guess costs nothing, so a blank only throws a chance away. Pearson's page says each question must be answered to receive results. Mark a guess, note the number on your scratch paper, and come back if the time's there.`},
        {t:`Stay on it till she can see how the circle fits.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She stayed on it. Two minutes, about, and she had it, and it was right, and it cost her two of the easy ones at the bottom that she never got to read.`,
          why:`One point, same as the easy one at the bottom of the page you never got to. You can't spend two minutes buying one problem when you've got twenty two for twenty five. I watched a lieutenant do this on a nav exam in 1974, on a star he couldn't find, and he never found the next three either. Guess, mark it, move.`},
        {t:`Flip to the back page, and work the rest from the end.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She flipped to the back. The back page was all circles and triangles, the kind she was slowest at. By the time she'd worked up to the middle, she'd lost track of which ones she'd done.`,
          why:`Jumping around costs you time you never see go. Nothing says the quick ones are waiting at the back, either. Go straight through and don't stall.`},
        {t:`Put her best guess down, circle the number, and go on.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`She put B. It looked about right for corners that size. She circled fourteen on her scratch sheet and went on. Fifteen was a straight line through two points, and she had it in thirty seconds.`,
          why:`That's pace, Sergeant. A guess on the slow one, a mark so you can find it, and on to the next. A guess costs you nothing. A blank never helped anybody. If there's time left over at the end, go back to your circles. The corners are the square less the circle: side squared, minus pi times the radius squared, and the radius is half the side. A side of 10 is 100 less about 78.5, so a little over 21 for all four corners. On Math Knowledge you get about fifty three seconds a problem, and no single problem's worth two of them.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s3`, domain:`Science and Aviation`, task:`PS`, title:`Same depth`, q:`Same depth of water, one tank narrow and one wide. Where's the pressure at the bottom higher?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const st=(G||{}).skippedtimer,p1=((s||{}).picks||{})[1];return [p1===0?(st?`When the timer went she was on nineteen, with six problems on the back page she'd never read. She filled them in with C while Graham said the one word, and he waited till her pencil stopped before he took the packet. Fourteen was still blank. She'd never gotten back to it.`:`When the timer went she was on twenty five, and fourteen was still blank. She'd meant to get back to it. Graham said the one word and waited till her pencil stopped before he took the packet.`):p1===1?`When the timer went, fourteen was right, and the bottom of the page wasn't. She filled the last ${st?`eight`:`two`} in with C while Graham said the one word.`:p1===2?`When the timer went she had answers scattered over both pages and couldn't have said which ones she'd done. She filled every empty circle she could find with C while Graham said the one word.`:st?`When the timer went she was on nineteen, with six problems on the back page she'd never read. She filled them in with C while Graham said the one word, and he waited till her pencil stopped before he took the packet. Fourteen still had her B on it, circled. She'd never gotten back to it.`:`When the timer went she was back on fourteen. The circle had finally opened, and she changed her B to a D with about two seconds left. She didn't know yet whether that was smart.`,
        `Reading went by in twenty four minutes. Situational Judgment took thirty five, a stack of young lieutenants with problems that weren't hers, and she found she knew what Vern would say about most of them. Then Graham set the timer for fifteen and pointed at the fridge, which meant food. She ate a sandwich standing up and didn't look at her coat.`,
        `Where the inventory went, Graham had left forty five minutes on the clock and nothing to answer, because on the day she'd be that tired. She sat through them with her eyes shut and heard every car go by on Orchard Street.`,
        `Then PHYSICAL SCIENCE, twenty in ten minutes. Thirty seconds each. Graham taught this five days a week, and in nine years she'd never once asked him to teach her any of it.`,
        `Number six showed two open tanks of water filled to the same depth. One was tall and narrow. The other was as wide as a barn. Graham had drawn them himself, with a little cartoon cow by the wide one. For scale, she guessed.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Neither. The depth sets it, not the width.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Neither. It came so fast it surprised her. Out at the dead end of Cooper Road, the pressure was whatever the tower on the hill made it. Not how much water. How high. Two point three one feet to the pound, and she'd done that one at three in the morning more times than she'd ever tell anybody. Marked. Most of her thirty seconds still left for seven.`,
          why:`Depth. And how dense the liquid is. That's all pressure in a liquid cares about. Never width. Wide tank, more water, sure. It's spread over more floor. Each square inch gets the same as the skinny one's. About a pound per square inch every 2.31 feet of fresh water, and you knew that one before I did. The miss is more water, more pressure. I heard a city councilman in Kansas City stand up and argue for a fatter water tower once, to get his ward more pressure. Nobody had the heart.`},
        {t:`The wide one. There's more water in it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She marked the wide one. More water, more push, she figured. Then she saw the tower on the hill in her head, skinny as a grain leg and feeding the whole east side, and changed it with four seconds to go.`,
          why:`More water, more total weight, sure. But it's spread over more bottom. Pressure's per square inch, and depth's what sets it.`},
        {t:`The same, since water weighs the same per gallon.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She marked the same. Right answer. Wrong reason, and she knew it a second later. Weight per gallon was density. It mattered. It was the same in both tanks, though, so it couldn't be what made them match. The depth did that.`,
          why:`Right answer, half a reason. Density matters. Salt water pushes harder than fresh at the same depth. But both tanks hold the same water, and what makes them equal is the same depth. Get the reason right and the next one, where the depths differ, won't fool you.`},
        {t:`The narrow one. There's less floor to spread it on.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Less floor. She marked the narrow one. It was backward. Less floor, sure, and less water sitting on it, same proportion. The clock ran out her thirty seconds before she saw it.`,
          why:`Less floor, but less water on it, in the same proportion. The two cancel. Depth is the only thing left standing, and the depth's the same.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s4`, domain:`Speed and Spatial`, task:`TR`, title:`Minus four, plus seven`, q:`Her number isn't one of the five choices. What should Kit do about it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Then TABLE READING. One grid the size of the whole page. Columns ran minus ten to ten across the top, rows ran ten down to minus ten along the side, plus numbers on top. Forty questions in seven minutes. Graham had run the grid off on the copier in the teachers' lounge, and it had a gray stripe down the middle where the glass was dirty. She put her left hand flat on the table and her pencil in her right.`,
        `Number twenty two asked for X minus 4, Y plus 7. She went across the top to minus 4, ran down, and got 63. The choices were 61, 64, 66, 67 and 58. Sixty three wasn't any of them.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Take 64, the one closest to what she found.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She took 64. It sat one cell over from the right answer, and it was wrong, and she'd have bet money on it.`,
          why:`You knew something had slipped, and you were right to stop. But close isn't a reason. The wrong choices are often cells right around the true one, so near is exactly where the traps live. Find it again from the labels.`},
        {t:`Run the same path again, only slower.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She ran it again, slower. Across to minus 4, down from the zero row, seven more. Sixty three again. She'd done the same wrong thing twice, carefully.`,
          why:`If the path was wrong, slower just walks you down it again. I've watched airmen torque the wrong bolt twice, very carefully. Start over from the labels at the edge of the grid.`},
        {t:`Go back to the edge labels and find it again.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`She took her pencil back to the edges. X across the top, minus 4. Y down the side, plus 7, up near the top, where Graham had put the plus numbers. She'd run down from the zero row and landed on minus 7. The cell at minus 4, plus 7 said 66, right there in the choices, two over from the 64 she'd almost taken.`,
          why:`When your number isn't there, you slipped. You didn't find a misprint. Go back to the labels, not to the cell you were staring at. X runs across the top, Y down the side, and check which way the side runs, plus at the top or at the bottom. The usual slips are a swapped X and Y, and a lost minus sign. Accuracy first. On this subtest you get about ten seconds a question, and that's enough to find a cell twice.`},
        {t:`Leave it, and save the ten seconds for the next one.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She left it. On a forty question block that's one point gone for good, and she'd had the time to find it twice.`,
          why:`Skipping on a speed test is sometimes right. Not here. You were one look from it. A number that isn't in the choices usually means a slip you can fix in five seconds.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s5`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`The paper on the table`, q:`The sheet's on the table between them. What does Kit say to him?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const tn=(G||{}).toldnoone;return [`Instrument Comprehension came and went in five minutes, the dials she'd frozen on in 2019, and she didn't freeze. She didn't do well, either, and she knew it as she went. Block Counting, then Aviation Information, and a little before noon Graham said it for the last time.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Even, plain, done.">"Time."</span>`,
        `He squared the eleven packets into a pile, took the binder clips off and put them in his shirt pocket, and slid the stack into his school bag. Then he kissed the top of her head and went out to mow the side yard, which didn't need it.`,
        ...(tn?[`She slept two hours on the couch. When she woke, Graham was at the kitchen table with the winter coats piled on the chair beside him, hers on top. First warm Saturday, so he'd stripped the hooks for a wash. He went through every pocket before a wash, every coat, the way his mother had taught him, and it was the only going through he'd ever done. Vern's log lay open on the table in front of him, and on top of it, unfolded, the sheet.`,
          `Pilot 19. Under it, in somebody else's capitals, ACUITY. <span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Quiet, careful, unsure.">"This was in your coat."</span> He'd read it standing up, she could tell, all the way to the bottom, and then he'd sat down.`]
          :[`She slept two hours on the couch. At six she made eggs, because eggs were what they had, and when the plates were in the sink she went to the hook by the door and took the sheet out of her coat.`,
          `She unfolded it on the kitchen table, under the light, where she'd taken the test that morning. Pilot 19. Under it, in Vern's capitals, ACUITY. Graham sat down across from her, in the same chair he'd timed her from, and waited.`])]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Start with that winter: she was twenty five, the inspection was real, and Boyd really asked.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"I was twenty five. The inspection was real, Graham. Boyd really did ask me."</span> Every word of it was true. She heard it come out in the old order, the reasons first and the thing they stood in front of last, and she watched him hear it that way too.`,
          why:`Every word true, and you lined them up as a defense before he'd said a thing. Integrity and Communication both lean on the plain fact first. The reasons can come later, if he asks for them. Lead with them and they sound like what they've always been, a front.`},
        {t:`Tell him she's sorry first, and ask him not to be angry.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"I'm sorry. I need you not to be angry before I say it."</span> He looked at her a long time. She'd asked him to carry it before she'd even set it down.`,
          why:`You asked him to forgive something he hadn't heard yet. That puts him to work before he knows what the work is. Say the thing first. What he does with it is his.`},
        {t:`Say it in her own words: the nineteen, the yes, and what she let him think.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, plain, getting it out.">"It wasn't Boyd. The scores came back that fall, and Pilot was nineteen, and it has to be twenty five. Then Boyd asked me to stay for the inspection, and I said yes. Yes meant I never had to say nineteen to you. I told you he'd asked, and I let that be the reason."</span> She stopped there. She'd thought there would be more, something about being twenty five, and there wasn't.`,
          why:`Integrity. The plain kind. The fact first, in your own words, nothing stacked in front of it. You didn't ask him for a thing. Not forgiveness, not patience. On the test, the action that owns the problem flat out and leaves the other fellow his own reaction is where the key leans. Second time you've told it in the right order. It doesn't get shorter.`},
        {t:`Point at the numbers and let the paper say it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She put her finger under the nineteen and waited. He read it. CSO meant nothing to him, and neither did nineteen. He didn't know whose word that was in the margin, or why it was there. The paper couldn't tell him any of it, and she sat there and let it try.`,
          why:`Paper doesn't talk. He needed it in your voice, at your own table. Sliding a paper across a desk and then sitting there is what a scared airman does with a first sergeant, and you've been the other chair for that one.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s6`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Seven years`, q:`He's said it, and he's waiting. What does Kit do now?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const tn=(G||{}).toldnoone,p4=((s||{}).picks||{})[4];return [...(p4===1||p4===3?[`She said it anyway, after that. The nineteen, the twenty five, the yes. He'd heard all of it before he said a word.`]:[]),
        `For a long time Graham didn't say anything. He picked up the sheet and read it again${tn?`, slower this time`:`, all the way to the bottom, same as he'd read a lab report`}. Then he put one finger on the word in the margin, the way he'd point at a wrong answer, only gentler. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Whose writing is that? It isn't yours."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Vern's. It's his word, and it's his to tell."</span> He nodded as if that was the one fair thing on the table, and he set the sheet down square with the edge.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Quiet, level, careful.">"In January, when you told me you wanted it, I took a minute before I said okay. You noticed. I was doing some math of my own. I'd made my peace with the slot before I opened my mouth."</span>`,
        `${tn?``:`<span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Quiet, level, careful.">"I've had three weeks to guess what that 2019 was. I didn't guess this."</span> `}<span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Low, raw, steady.">"It's not the slot. You let me think it was Boyd for seven years."</span>`,
        `He looked at the dark window over the sink. <span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Low, rueful, tired.">"I used to hold it against him a little. Every Christmas party. There's the guy."</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him "I did," and stay put at the table.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, slow, owning it.">"I did."</span> She didn't add the part about the inspection being real. She didn't get up. He looked at the window. She let him. The clock over the stove was loud enough to count. After a while he said Boyd's name once more, not to her, and shook his head.`,
          why:`You owned it in two words, and then you stayed in the room for what it did to him. No defense, no fix, no leaving him alone with it. Integrity's the competency here, and the key leans hard toward owning a thing outright over explaining it. The staying is the part people skip.`},
        {t:`Ask him, gently, what he needs from her right now.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"What do you need from me?"</span> He thought about it honestly, the way he did everything. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"I don't know yet. Let me be mad a minute."</span> It was a fair question. It was a minute early.`,
          why:`Right instinct, and a good question. It came too soon. He'd had the truth about two minutes, and asking what he needs right then hands him a job. Let him have his reaction first. Ask tomorrow.`},
        {t:`Remind him there's a crib in the garage he never told her about.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"You've had a crib under a tarp for two years, Graham."</span> She heard it land. It was true, and she'd said it to make them even, and they both knew that was why.`,
          why:`You took his secret and used it as a counterweight. That turns your confession into a trade. On the test, answering somebody's grievance with one of your own lands near the bottom.`},
        {t:`Go up to bed, and give him the kitchen to himself tonight.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`She went up. It felt like kindness on the stairs. Down in the kitchen she heard him sit a long time, and then the back door, and then nothing for an hour.`,
          why:`It looks like giving him room. It's leaving him alone with it at the worst minute. Stay in the room until he tells you he wants it to himself. He hadn't.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s7`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Under the tarp`, q:`He's asked her for no more afters. What does Kit tell him?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p=(((all||{})[6]||{}).picks||{})[11],tn=(G||{}).toldnoone;return [`Near ten he got up, turned on the side light, and stood at the back door with his hand on the knob. <span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Quiet, level, tired.">"Come out to the garage a minute."</span>`,
        p===0?(tn?`She'd asked him about it Friday, and he'd said after the test. The test was done, and so was the rest of it.`:`She'd asked him about it Friday, and he'd said after hers. Hers was done now.`):p===2?`Friday she'd stood out there with her phone in her hand, about to call her mother about it.`:p===3?`Friday night she'd told herself she hadn't seen anything, and she'd kept on telling herself.`:`Friday she'd put the tarp back the way it had been, and left it for him to lift.`,
        `The motion light came on over them. He went past the mower to the back wall and took hold of the corner of the blue tarp, and before he could lift it, she said it. ${p===0?`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"You don't have to show me. I know what it is."</span>`:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"I saw it Friday, when I came out for the battery. I put the tarp back."</span>`}`,
        `He lifted the corner anyway and folded it back over the top rail, and the crib stood there in the light with its paper tag still tied to the post.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Quiet, steady, finally saying it.">"Two springs ago I stood right there in that door and asked you if this was the year we tried for a baby. You said after the KC-46 talk. That Saturday there was an estate sale out on Mill Road. Forty dollars. I didn't even haggle."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"After your degree. After the inspection. After the deployment. After the new jets."</span> He stopped on the second one. <span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Low, flat, hurt.">"The inspection was the lie too."</span>`,
        `It wasn't a question, and she didn't make him ask it. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Yes."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Quiet, plain, asking.">"I'm not asking you for an answer tonight. I'm asking you not to hand me another after."</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him yes, this year, and see how the rest falls out.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Yes. This year."</span> His face did something she wanted to keep. Then she watched him do the math, the test in May and the board in July and pilot training after that, and his face stopped doing it. It was a yes because yes was easier. She knew that sound. She'd invented it.`,
          why:`A yes because yes is easier is how you got here. You'd say it to end the hard minute, and he'd find out later what it was worth. Say what's true, even when it's less than he wants.`},
        {t:`Tell him it's his call, and she'll go along with whatever he wants.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Whatever you want. It's your call."</span> He shook his head. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"It isn't mine. That's the whole thing."</span>`,
          why:`Handing it to him sounds generous. It's one more way of not saying what you want. Some decisions belong to two people, and you just tried to make this one his.`},
        {t:`Tell him after the board, in July, and mean it this time.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"After the board. July. I mean it."</span> He heard the word before he heard the month. He nodded, because it was the best after she'd ever given him, with a date on it. It was still an after.`,
          why:`A date is better than none, and you'd likely keep it. But he asked you for one thing, no more afters, and you handed him the best one you've got. Answer what was asked.`},
        {t:`Tell him she doesn't know yet, and she won't say after.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, sure, unguarded.">"I don't know yet. I want to fly, Graham. I don't know how this fits next to that, and I'm not going to put a word on it to make tonight easier."</span> He looked at the crib a while. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Okay."</span> No beat in front of it, and no after behind it. He put the tarp corner back where it had been, and on the way out he switched off the light by the door, though it would've gone off by itself.`,
          why:`Integrity and Communication together. He asked for no more afters and you didn't give him one, and you didn't give him a yes you couldn't stand behind either. You told him the true state of things, unfinished as it is. The test rewards the honest answer over the comfortable one, and so do the people you'll lead.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s8`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Kitty`, q:`Her mother's text is still sitting there from yesterday. What does Kit do about her?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const tn=(G||{}).toldnoone;return [tn?`Sunday the house was quiet the way it got after a storm went through. The whiteboard said what it had said yesterday.`:`Sunday morning the 2019 was gone off the whiteboard. There was a gray smear in Saturday's square where a thumb had been, and the green around it was left alone.`,
        `Graham went to the hardware store at nine and came back at noon with a box of deck screws and nothing else. He was kind to her all morning, careful about it, the way people are kind in a hospital hallway.`,
        `Her mother had texted Saturday afternoon. Kit read it again after lunch, on the back step.`,
        `<prop type="text" from="Rhonda" time="2:41 PM">How did the big practice test go Kitty?? Darlene says five hours is longer than her wedding. Call me</prop>`,
        `Rhonda still had the Boyd version. She'd had it for seven years, and she'd told it at her own table, proud of a daughter who stayed for her people. Boyd was next, at drill.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Text back that it went fine, and leave the rest where it is.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`She texted that it went fine, long day, love you. Rhonda sent back a heart. The Boyd version went on living at her mother's house for one more week.`,
          why:`True as far as it goes, and it keeps the old story alive on purpose. Every week you leave it standing, it's harder to take back.`},
        {t:`Call her, and ask for an hour at her table next Sunday.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`She called. Rhonda picked up on the first ring, the way she always did, as if she'd had the phone in her hand the whole time. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"It went fine, Mom. Long. I'll have the numbers this week."</span> Then, before she could talk herself out of it: <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Can I come by next Sunday, when drill lets out? About four. There's something from 2019 I should've told you. I want to say it at your table."</span> The line went quiet. <span class="said" data-who="Rhonda" data-tone="Quiet, worried, careful.">"Is it bad?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"It's old."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Rhonda" data-tone="Warm, brisk, decided.">"Then I'll make a roast,"</span> her mother said, and Kit laughed for the first time since Friday.`,
          why:`Communication, with a time on it. She hears it from you, in person, and soon, and she knows it's coming, so it doesn't reach her from somebody else first. Boyd still hears it before she does, and that's the right order. He's the one who carried it.`},
        {t:`Call her now, and tell her all of it over the phone.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She called and told her all of it, on the back step, in about four minutes. Rhonda listened, said Kitty twice, and then asked whether Boyd knew, and Kit had to say not yet. Her mother had it before the man who'd paid on it for seven years.`,
          why:`Right to tell her, and right to tell her yourself. Wrong order and wrong place. The man who's carried the debt should hear it before anybody else does, and your mother deserved her own kitchen for it, not a phone on a back step.`},
        {t:`Ask Graham to call her, so she won't have to say it twice.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She asked him. Graham looked at her as if she'd asked him to sign her name for her. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"No."</span> He didn't say anything else, and he didn't need to.`,
          why:`That's sending a messenger with your own confession. It's yours to say. On the test, handing your hard conversation to somebody else lands near the bottom.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s9`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Science and Aviation`, task:`PS`, title:`Same gallons`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday at Vern's she said it with her coat still on. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Graham knows."</span> Vern looked at her over the percolator. The index card still stood against it, where it had stood for four weeks.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Vern" data-tone="Quiet, plain, warm.">"That's one."</span> He didn't ask how it went. At the end of the night he handed her the week's log, and she read it at the stop sign by the grain elevator with the dome light on.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Navigator's log, January 2001" by="Vern" say="My log, twenty two January, oh one. Minus eighteen Celsius on the ramp. Trucks metered twenty thousand gallons. Gauges said one hundred thirty eight thousand pounds. The same gallons in August made one thirty two. Did not argue with the gauges.">22 JAN 01. MINUS 18 C ON THE RAMP.<br>TRUCKS METERED 20,000 GAL.<br>GAUGES 138,000 LB.<br>SAME GALLONS IN AUGUST MADE 132,000.<br>DID NOT ARGUE WITH THE GAUGES.</prop>`,
        `Graham had put it on the whiteboard back in February, before any of this, one of her blue blocks: WED, MY LAB, 3:30. She hadn't known if it still stood, and she'd decided she wouldn't ask. At three on Wednesday he texted her the room number, as if she might not know it.`,
        `Room 114 smelled like the gas taps and a hundred sophomores. He'd set up three stations down the long black bench and pulled a stool up to the first one for her. His school bag was on the floor by his desk, and she could see her eleven packets in it under a stack of lab reports. He hadn't scored them.`,
        `He saw her see the bag. <span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Quiet, even, not looking up.">"Third period took a unit test Monday. Eleven of them came in under half."</span> He was setting two cylinders out on the bench and didn't look up from them. <span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Quiet, plain, hurt underneath.">"Some of them cried. Then they came in at lunch and we went through it, one at a time. You could've brought me a nineteen."</span> She nodded. Then she showed him Vern's log, because it was what she'd brought.`,
        `He read it and handed it back. <span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Dry, interested, careful.">"Your navigator didn't argue with the gauges. Good for him. Same gallons, six thousand more pounds. Where did they come from?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The fuel shrank in the cold, so each gallon held more fuel.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`She said it the way she'd have said it on the ramp. Same stuff, packed tighter. Graham nodded once. He got a bottle of cooking oil out of the lab fridge and warmed half of it on the hot plate, and she watched him measure a hundred milliliters of each, slower than he needed to. On the balance the cold one came out about three grams heavier. She thought of the hose in January, so cold it bit through her gloves. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Density. Nobody added any fuel. Same fuel, smaller space."</span>`,
          why:`Density. Mass over volume. Cool almost anything and it pulls in a little, same mass in less room, so a gallon of it weighs more. That's why I planned fuel in pounds and never gallons, twenty one years at the nav table and thirty one at dispatch. Pounds are what the wings carry. Jet fuel runs about 6.6 pounds a gallon on an August afternoon, closer to 6.9 on a January morning, and the truck driver doesn't care which. I watched a new dispatcher forget it once and have a captain on the phone in about four minutes, and the captain was not a patient man. Where folks miss is thinking the cold added mass. Mass never changed. The room it took up did.`},
        {t:`It's thicker cold, so a gallon of it weighs more.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Thicker, she said. Graham tipped the cold cylinder so she could watch how slow it poured. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"That's thick, all right. Thick is how slow it pours. Ever see motor oil sitting on a puddle in the shop? Thick, and lighter than the water. Heavy for its size is something else."</span>`,
          why:`Right direction, wrong word. Cold fuel's thicker and it's heavier by the gallon. Two facts, not one. Pouring slow is viscosity. How heavy it is for its size is density. Motor oil's thick, and it still floats on a puddle. They'll hand you both words the same morning, five choices apart.`},
        {t:`The cold put weight into it, so cold fuel has more mass.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Then where'd the extra mass come from? The truck?"</span> He wasn't being smart with her. He honestly wanted to know, and she didn't have an answer, because there wasn't one.`,
          why:`Mass doesn't come out of the weather. Twenty thousand cold gallons hold more fuel than twenty thousand warm ones, but nobody made any fuel that morning. The same mass took up less room, that's all. Temperature moves the volume. Density tags along behind like a dog.`},
        {t:`The cold throws the gauges off, and she wouldn't trust them.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She said it like a crew chief. Graham let that sit a second. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Your navigator trusted them. So does the airplane."</span> He put the two cylinders on the balance, and the gauges, it turned out, had been fine.`,
          why:`Checking an instrument's a good habit, and I'd never talk you out of it. I wrote up a fuel gauge myself in 1983 that turned out to be telling the truth. Cold fuel is denser. When a reading makes physical sense, it isn't the gauge's fault.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s10`, domain:`Science and Aviation`, task:`PS`, title:`Two syringes`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Station two was a pair of plastic syringes with a foot of clear tubing between them and blue water through the whole thing. The skinny one was what you'd use to get a pill into a cat. The fat one she could've stuck her thumb in. Masking tape on each in his printing, 1 SQ CM and 5 SQ CM, on straight, which with Graham meant he'd had time on his hands. He hadn't said her name once since three thirty.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Easy, plain, teaching.">"Your truck's brakes. You push a little piston at the pedal, and there's a fat one out at every wheel, with brake fluid between. The jet's the same, except a pump does your pushing, and you'd know how loud."</span> He put two fingers on the skinny one. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Say I push this one in ten centimeters with twenty newtons. What's the fat one do?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Pushes with twenty newtons. Same pressure, same push.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Same pressure, sure, and she'd stopped right there. Graham tapped the masking tape on the fat one with his pen, twice. Five square centimeters. Five times the floor for it to push on, though.`,
          why:`Half right, and on this test half right's a wrong answer. Pressure's the same everywhere in that water. Force isn't. Force is pressure times area, and the fat one's got five times the area to push on. Circle the word force when you see it, every time. I circled everything at nav school, and the instructor said my pages looked like the dartboard at the VFW.`},
        {t:`Pushes with a hundred newtons, and moves the same ten centimeters.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`A hundred newtons, right. Then he stood a ruler by the fat plunger and pushed again. Two centimeters, and it quit. <span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Dry, amused, warm.">"If it went ten, I'd retire."</span>`,
          why:`Got the force. Skipped the bill. Five times the force over the same ten centimeters would be five times the work out of one push, and if that worked I'd have bought a boat and named it after Pascal. And a fifth as far. Always.`},
        {t:`Pushes with four newtons, and moves fifty centimeters.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She'd flipped it. Graham pushed the skinny one again and the fat one crawled up. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"That look like fifty to you?"</span> It looked like two.`,
          why:`Upside down. Fat piston, big force, short trip. The bottle jack behind your truck seat. You pump and pump on that skinny handle and the truck comes up a hair a stroke.`},
        {t:`Pushes with a hundred newtons, and moves two centimeters.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Twenty newtons on one square centimeter, and that same squeeze all through the blue water. The fat one had five square centimeters for it to push on, so a hundred newtons. A fifth as far. It climbed two centimeters, right on the mark, and the blue in the tube shivered. He worked it on a paper towel from the dispenser with a ballpoint, which tore it. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"A hundred times two. Twenty times ten. Same work, both ends of the tube. Water doesn't give you anything for free."</span>`,
          why:`That's Pascal. Squeeze a shut in fluid anywhere and you've squeezed it everywhere, same amount. Pressure's force over area. Fat piston, five times the area, five times the shove. And it goes a fifth as far. That's the bill. It always comes. Folks grab the force and forget the bill. Two two eight's gear comes up on that rule every flight. Has since Kennedy. Works because water hardly squeezes. Put a bubble in the line and you're pushing on a sponge, and you know that better than I do.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s11`, domain:`Science and Aviation`, task:`PS`, title:`Two roads`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Station three was the one he liked, she could tell. Plywood, a battery pack out of the supply closet, two bulbs in screw sockets, red and black clip leads all over. Somebody'd written 12 V on the pack in Sharpie, and somebody else had added a smiley face. Gum under the bench lip, gray and old. She'd have bet a week's pay he knew whose.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Call each bulb six ohms. Close enough for government work."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Side by side, each one with its own road home. That's parallel."</span> Last clip, and both bulbs lit up hard enough to make her squint. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"So. How much current's coming out of that battery?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Two amps: twelve volts over six ohms.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Two amps was right for one bulb. Graham pointed at the other one. <span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Dry, plain, amused.">"And that one's running on what, hope?"</span>`,
          why:`You worked one branch and quit on the other. That battery's feeding both. I did the same with fuel once, two receivers on the schedule and I counted one, and the boom operator caught it before I did. He brought it up at every Christmas party till he retired.`},
        {t:`One amp: twelve volts over twelve ohms.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She'd added the sixes. He moved two clips so the bulbs sat one after the other in a single loop, and both went dim and orange. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"One amp, and that's series. You wired it in series in your head."</span>`,
          why:`You added the resistances, and that's series. Look at the wires before you do any arithmetic, and then look again, same as you would safety wire.`},
        {t:`Four amps, two through each of the paths.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Each road got the whole twelve volts, so two amps a road, and two roads. Graham unclipped one bulb. The other one didn't so much as flicker. She kept her eyes on the one still lit. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"That's your house, more or less. Every outlet gets the full hundred and twenty. The toaster doesn't care if the lamp's on."</span>`,
          why:`That's parallel, and he set it up so you'd see it. Each branch gets all twelve volts, so you work them one at a time with Ohm's law and add. Twelve over six is two, and two branches makes four. Taken together the two bulbs come out three ohms, lower than either one, since there's a second road now. I sat on the Kansas City beltway enough evenings to know what one more lane does at five o'clock. If you got twelve, you added six and six. That's series, one road through both, one amp, and a couple of dim orange bulbs.`},
        {t:`Seventy two amps: twelve times six.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Graham waited to see if she'd hear it. She did. Seventy two amps out of a lab battery would have melted his clips.`,
          why:`Volts times ohms isn't anything you'll ever want. Ohm's law is volts equal amps times ohms, so amps are volts over ohms. Draw the little triangle on your scratch paper, V on top. I drew it on my hand once before a check ride.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s12`, domain:`Science and Aviation`, task:`PS`, title:`Red pen`, q:`Eleven numbers in red pen on the cover page. How should Kit read them?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const st=(G||{}).skippedtimer;return [`Friday morning she came off nights at ten after seven. The house was empty. Graham had left for school.`,
        `The coffee was on, the pot about half full and starting to smell burnt, which meant he'd made it for her and not for himself. On the kitchen table, squared to the edge, were her eleven packets, every page marked in his red pen. Sometime after she'd left for the plant last night, he'd sat down at this table and done all of it.`,
        `On the top packet was a yellow sticky note in his printing.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Sticky note" by="Graham" say="A R, twenty one out of twenty five. Good.">AR 21/25. Good.</prop>`,
        `Under it, on the cover page, he'd written every block's number in a column, the way he wrote grades in his book.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Practice Test 1, scored" by="Graham" say="Verbal Analogies, twenty of twenty five. Arithmetic Reasoning, twenty one of twenty five. Word Knowledge, nineteen of twenty five. Math Knowledge, ${st?`fifteen`:`twenty`} of twenty five. Reading, twenty of twenty five. Situational Judgment, twenty five of thirty two. Physical Science, thirteen of twenty. Table Reading, thirty three of forty. Instrument Comprehension, fourteen of twenty five. Block Counting, twenty two of thirty. Aviation Information, eleven of twenty.">VA 20/25<br>AR 21/25<br>WK 19/25<br>MK ${st?`15`:`20`}/25<br>RC 20/25<br>SJ 25/32<br>PS 13/20<br>TR 33/40<br>IC 14/25<br>BC 22/30<br>AI 11/20</prop>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Look up a chart online that turns raw scores into percentiles.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She found three of them. They disagreed with each other by twenty points, and none of them said where its numbers had come from.`,
          why:`Nobody outside the real test can turn your raw count into a percentile. Charts like that are guesses with a table drawn around them. Use a practice test to find your weak spots, not to predict your number.`},
        {t:`Block by block, against the four blocks Pilot leans on.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`She got a pen and went down the cover page against the four that Pilot leans on. Math Knowledge, Table Reading, Instrument Comprehension, Aviation Information. Table Reading was fine. Math she already knew about. Instruments was fourteen of twenty five, and Aviation was eleven of twenty, on a jet she'd been crewing since she was twenty two. She opened the Aviation packet to see what she'd missed, and it was all from inside the airplane, every miss a thing a pilot would know and a crew chief wouldn't. She wrote IC and AI at the top of a clean page and boxed them twice.`,
          why:`Read a practice score where it counts. Pilot leans on Math Knowledge, Table Reading, Instrument Comprehension and Aviation Information, so those four tell you the most about the composite you need. Then open a low one and look for the pattern in the misses. Real percentiles come only from the real test, so don't try to turn raw numbers into one. Physical Science feeds no composite in the 2015 pamphlet. Work on it, but not first.`},
        {t:`Add it all up, and see how far she's come since 2019.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She added it up in the margin and got a number out of two hundred ninety two. She tried to set it next to the sheet in her coat and couldn't. One was raw points on a test her husband built. The other was percentiles from the real thing.`,
          why:`Wanting to know how far you've come is fair, and the total says you've worked. It can't say where. A raw total from a practice test doesn't line up with percentiles from the real one, and it hides the two blocks that'll decide your Pilot number.`},
        {t:`Drill Physical Science first, since Graham can teach it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`It was the easy one to plan, with a physics teacher in the house. She had four sessions blocked out before she noticed she'd planned around the block that counted least toward Pilot.`,
          why:`Convenient isn't the same as useful. Physical Science feeds no composite in the 2015 pamphlet, and Pilot doesn't lean on it in any version. Spend the most time where the composite you need is weakest.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f7s11`, title:`Graham's cards`, intro:`When the stations were put away, Graham took a rubber banded stack of index cards out of his desk drawer, the ones he used on review days, and dealt her eight off the top. Same rules the sophomores got: three answers where the real test has five, pick one, and not a word out of him till she'd done all eight.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`A wheelbarrow carries its load between the wheel and your hands. What class of lever is it?`, choices:[`First class`,`Second class`,`Third class`], answer:1, why:`Load in the middle is second class. Axle's the fulcrum. Your hands are the effort, way out at the end. You lift more than you push.`, task:`PS`},
      {prompt:`Which change of state gives off heat to whatever's around it?`, choices:[`Condensation`,`Evaporation`,`Melting`], answer:0, why:`Gas going back to liquid gives up the heat it took to boil it. That's why steam off a pot burns you worse than the water in it. Evaporating and melting go the other way and soak heat up. Ask anybody who's worked a ramp in a wet shirt in March.`, task:`PS`},
      {prompt:`What holds the hydrogen and oxygen in a water molecule together?`, choices:[`Shared electrons, a covalent bond`,`Electrons handed over, an ionic bond`,`Magnetism`], answer:0, why:`Two nonmetals share. Handing electrons over is ionic, a metal and a nonmetal, like table salt.`, task:`PS`},
      {prompt:`Day and night come from Earth's`, choices:[`Revolution around the sun`,`Rotation on its axis`,`Tilted axis`], answer:1, why:`Spin, a day. Going around the sun's the year. The tilt's your seasons.`, task:`PS`},
      {prompt:`A magnifying glass is a lens that is`, choices:[`Thinner in the middle, and spreads light out`,`Flat on both sides`,`Thicker in the middle, and brings light together`], answer:2, why:`Convex, fat in the middle, bends light in toward a point. Concave spreads it.`, task:`PS`},
      {prompt:`A 10 tooth gear drives a 30 tooth gear. The big gear turns`, choices:[`Three times as fast, with less force`,`A third as fast, with more turning force`,`At the same speed`], answer:1, why:`Small driving big trades speed for force. Three turns of the little one make one turn of the big one.`, task:`PS`},
      {prompt:`Water boils at 100 degrees Celsius at sea level. How many degrees Fahrenheit is that?`, choices:[`180`,`212`,`132`], answer:1, why:`Nine fifths of 100 is 180, plus 32. People stop at 180 and forget the 32.`, task:`PS`},
      {prompt:`A warm front usually brings`, choices:[`A quick line of thunderstorms, then clearing`,`Clear, dry, sinking air`,`Low gray clouds and long, steady rain`], answer:2, why:`Warm air slides up over cold slowly, so the weather's slow too. That quick line of storms is a cold front coming through. Clear sinking air's a high.`, task:`PS`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`A hydraulic lift's large piston has 12 times the area of its small piston. The small piston is pushed down 36 cm. How far does the large piston rise?`, opts:[`3 cm`,`36 cm`,`432 cm`,`12 cm`,`24 cm`], a:0, why:`Water leaving the small side has to go somewhere. On the big side it spreads over 12 times the floor, so the big piston comes up a twelfth as far. Thirty six over 12, 3 centimeters, and it pushes 12 times harder for its trouble. 432 multiplied where it should've divided. That's the free work machine, and the man at the county fair in 1964 is still the only one who ever claimed to have one.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`A 600 N load sits 0.4 m from a pry bar's fulcrum. How hard must you push down 2 m from the fulcrum on the other side to lift it?`, opts:[`3,000 N`,`120 N`,`240 N`,`300 N`,`600 N`], a:1, why:`Effort times its distance equals load times its distance. Six hundred times 0.4 is 240, and 240 over 2 is 120. A lot of people stop at 240, one step short, and I've watched them do it with the pencil still moving. 3,000 turned the ratio upside down.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`A block and tackle has 5 strands of rope holding up its moving block. Ignoring friction, about how much pull will lift a 1,000 N engine?`, opts:[`1,000 N`,`500 N`,`200 N`,`5,000 N`,`250 N`], a:2, why:`Five strands share that engine, a fifth apiece, so two hundred newtons. You'll haul five meters of rope for every meter it comes up. 250 counted four strands, and 500 counted pulleys.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`Two skaters stand still on smooth ice. One is 60 kg and the other 40 kg. They push off each other, and the 40 kg skater glides away at 3 m/s. How fast does the 60 kg skater move?`, opts:[`3 m/s`,`4.5 m/s`,`0 m/s`,`2 m/s`,`1.2 m/s`], a:3, why:`No momentum to start, so none in total at the end. Forty times 3 is 120 one way, so the 60 kilogram skater carries 120 the other way, which is 2 meters a second. 4.5 flipped the ratio. Zero says the forces cancel. They don't. Each push lands on its own skater. Patrice and I tried this on a frozen pond in 1975 and she went farther, and she's never let me forget why.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`Two masses are moved three times as far apart. The gravitational pull between them becomes`, opts:[`one third as strong`,`three times as strong`,`nine times as strong`,`unchanged`,`one ninth as strong`], a:4, why:`Inverse square. Three times as far, three squared is nine, so a ninth of the pull. One third is what you get when you forget to square it, and it's sitting right there at the top waiting for you.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`It takes a 200 N push to start a heavy crate sliding across a concrete floor. Once it's moving, keeping it at a steady speed usually takes`, opts:[`more than 200 N`,`less than 200 N`,`exactly 200 N`,`no force at all`,`about 400 N`], a:1, why:`Friction on a thing sitting still usually runs higher than on a thing sliding, so once it's going it takes less. No force at all would only be true with no friction, and I've never met a concrete floor like that.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`About how much potential energy does a 5 kg box gain when it's lifted 3 m? (Use g as 10 m/s².)`, opts:[`15 J`,`50 J`,`150 J`,`8 J`,`1.5 J`], a:2, why:`Mass times g times height: 5 times 10 times 3, 150 joules. 15 forgot the g. 50 forgot the height. 8 added the numbers, which I've seen a tired man do at three in the morning.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`The sun's heat reaches Earth across about 150 million km of nearly empty space by`, opts:[`conduction through the thin gas of space`,`convection currents between the planets`,`light from the moon bouncing it here`,`sound waves from the sun's surface`,`radiation`], a:4, why:`Only radiation gets across empty space. Conduction needs things touching, convection needs a fluid to ride in, and sound needs something to travel through. Space has none of that, which is why it's so quiet up there, I'm told.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`A pot of ice and water sits on a burner and gets stirred. While ice is still melting, the water's temperature`, opts:[`stays at about 0 °C`,`rises steadily`,`drops below 0 °C`,`rises to 4 °C and holds there`,`jumps to 100 °C`], a:0, why:`The heat goes into melting the ice, and the thermometer sits at zero till the last of it's gone. Four degrees is where water's densest. True, and a different question.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`A sealed, rigid tank of gas is warmed from 27 °C to 177 °C. Its pressure becomes about`, opts:[`6.6 times as high`,`1.5 times as high`,`twice as high`,`the same as before`,`two thirds as high`], a:1, why:`Kelvins first. 27 Celsius is 300 kelvins and 177 is 450, so the pressure goes up by 450 over 300, one and a half times. 6.6 is 177 over 27. That's the Celsius trap, and it catches somebody every time.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`A 120 V space heater draws 12.5 A. How much power does it use?`, opts:[`9.6 W`,`132.5 W`,`150 W`,`1,500 W`,`15,000 W`], a:3, why:`Power's volts times amps: 120 times 12.5, 1,500 watts. 9.6 divided. 132.5 added. 150 and 15,000 lost track of the decimal.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`Which change makes an electromagnet stronger?`, opts:[`Using fewer turns of wire`,`Sending less current through the coil`,`Swapping the iron core for a plastic one`,`Reversing the battery`,`Adding more turns of wire to the coil`], a:4, why:`More turns, more current, or an iron core inside. Turning the battery around just swaps which end is north. The strength doesn't care.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`Sound travels about 1,500 m/s in seawater. A sonar pulse at 3,000 Hz has what wavelength?`, opts:[`2 m`,`1,500 m`,`4,500 m`,`0.5 m`,`4,500,000 m`], a:3, why:`Speed's frequency times wavelength. Turn it around, wavelength's speed over frequency. Fifteen hundred over three thousand, half a meter. 2 meters divided the wrong way up. 4,500,000 multiplied. That's a long wave.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`A beam of light strikes a flat mirror 35° from the normal, the line straight out from the mirror. At what angle from the normal does it leave?`, opts:[`35°`,`55°`,`70°`,`0°`,`145°`], a:0, why:`Same angle out as in, off the normal. Pool table, cue ball, no English, same thing. 55 got measured from the mirror's face. 70's the whole angle between the beams. Read what they're measuring from. I lost a dollar to a boom operator on that one.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`Elements that sit in the same column of the periodic table tend to`, opts:[`have the same atomic mass`,`have the same number of protons`,`be gases`,`behave alike chemically`,`be metals`], a:3, why:`Column's a group, and a group shares its outer electron count. That's why they act alike. The proton count climbs every step down. Can't be the same.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`Which of these is a mixture rather than a compound?`, opts:[`Table salt`,`Salt water`,`Water`,`Carbon dioxide`,`Baking soda`], a:1, why:`Salt water is two things together and not bonded, any amounts. Boil it off and the salt's back. The other four are compounds, each a fixed recipe of atoms hooked together.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`A neutral oxygen atom gains two extra electrons. What is it now?`, opts:[`a positive ion`,`a different element`,`a negative ion`,`a heavier isotope`,`a noble gas`], a:2, why:`Electrons carry the minus, so two extra make it an ion with a charge of minus two. Change the protons and it's another element. Change the neutrons, an isotope.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`What gives the moon its phases?`, opts:[`Earth's shadow on the moon`,`Clouds passing in front of it`,`The moon spinning faster some weeks`,`The sun dimming`,`The angle we see its lit half from`], a:4, why:`Half the moon's always lit by the sun, every night of the month. Some nights we see a lot of that half, some nights a sliver. Earth's shadow on the moon is an eclipse, and that would be on the evening news.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`A microsecond is`, opts:[`a thousandth of a second`,`a billionth of a second`,`a millionth of a second`,`a hundredth of a second`,`a million seconds`], a:2, why:`Micro is a millionth. Milli's a thousandth, nano a billionth, and mega would be the million. I'd write the ladder on my scratch paper before anything else.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`},
    {q:`Most of the air in Earth's atmosphere is`, opts:[`nitrogen`,`oxygen`,`carbon dioxide`,`water vapor`,`argon`], a:0, why:`About 78 percent nitrogen, 21 oxygen, and argon's most of the last 1. Folks guess oxygen, since that's the part they're breathing for. I did too, till a flight surgeon set me straight in 1975.`, task:`PS`, domain:`Science and Aviation`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `She read the sticky note again. AR 21/25. Good.`,
    `Graham wrote Good on a lab report when it was true. Not once when it wasn't. His sophomores had that figured out by October, and the smart ones by the second week. Every page of eleven packets, marked, at the same table she'd told him at. And the only word on any of it that wasn't a number was that one. Under the one block that earned it.`,
    `She read it a third time and still couldn't tell which part of the week it sat under.`,
    `She peeled the note off the packet and a little of the cover page came along. She thumbed it flat. Her coat was on the hook. In the inside pocket, same as always, the sheet, folded under Vern's two logs, ACUITY on the inside where nobody'd see it. She stuck Graham's yellow note on the outside of the fold. Next time that sheet came out of her pocket, his note would come out first.`,
    `She tore a sheet off his pad of yellow notes and wrote one of her own, in her printing, which was worse than his.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Sticky note" by="Kit" say="P S, thirteen out of twenty. Will you teach me the seven?">PS 13/20. Will you teach me the seven?</prop>`,
    `She stuck it on the coffee maker, over the switch. He'd be home at four and she'd be asleep, so he'd find it before he found her. She shut the pot off and poured what was left into the mug with the chipped handle. Six o'clock coffee at a quarter to eight. She drank it anyway, at the sink, boots still on.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch07.mp3`, len:`about 90 minutes`},
  next:`Inside the Jet`
});
